Always pelled out of the egg: Max Raabe turns 59
He is able to inspire his audience with reinterpretations of hitters from the 20s and 30s.
He is currently on the road with the program "Good day, dear happiness" - if he is allowed to perform.
Max Raabe would have wanted to celebrate his birthday on stage in Leipzig this Sunday.
But nothing becomes of it.
The planned concert has been postponed to May 2022 - "on official instruction" to contain the corona virus.
Others of his performances planned during the Advent season also have to be cancelled, as it says on the website of the singer, who turns 59 today, and his musicians (www.palast-orchester.de).
From January, the artists then want to make up for their appointments, which have already been postponed in 2020 due to the pandemic.
Born on 12 December 1962 in Lünen, North Rhine-Westphalia, Max Raabe sanged a lot already as a teenager.
He studied opera singing in Berlin. In 1986, together with fellow students, he founded the Palace Orchestra.
Since then, the baritone has established itself with its repertoire of hits and chansons in the style of comedian harmonists.
He has been awarded numerous prizes.
His live album from the "MTV Unplugged" series (2019) - just like his successful album "Für Frauen ist dies nicht Problem" (2013) - reached second place in the charts.
He's made it out of the niche into the mainstream.
Always dressed elegantly, the blonde hair strictly combed back and focused on etiquette, Max Raabe is an indispensable part of the current German-speaking music landscape.
He has been on tour since 2020 with the program "Good day, dear happiness". dpa
