Amazon founder Bezo sends astronaut daughter and presenter into space
The daughter of the first American in space, a TV moderator and four other passengers have taken a short trip into space.
They were transported on Saturday by "New Shepard" rocket system from Amazon founder Jeff Bezos.
Take-off and landing the approximately ten-minute flight took place in the west of the US state of Texas.
Under a bright blue sky, Bezos personally received the newly crowned astronauts after their return.
"Welcome back," Bezos said, dressed in a blue spacesuit as he opened the hatch to the capsule.
Under the applause of friends, family members and employees of Blue Origin, the six passengers gradually climbed out and hugged the Amazon founder.
According to Blue Origin, they were ten minutes and 13 seconds on the way, reaching an altitude of 107 kilometers.
74-year-old Laura Shepard Churchley, daughter Alan Shepard (1923-1998), after whom Blue Origin's rocket system is named in 1961) and 50-year-old TV presenter Michael Strahan were honorary guests of the company, the other four passengers had paid for their tickets: space engineer Dylan Taylor, investor Evan Dick, technology entrepreneur Lane Bess as well as his son.
How much they each paid for their ticket, Blue Origin did not disclose.
Laura Shepard Churchley commemorated her father's mission after the short flight.
He had to work hard during his assignments and do everything himself.
She had just flown, said the 74-year-old with a laugh.
Michael Strahan also expressed enthusiasm.
He'd like to get back to space right now, the moderator said.
"New Shepard" flies up to 100 kilometres
New Shepard missile system flies largely automatically.
The capsule, which was separated from the rocket during the flight, reached an altitude of around 100 kilometers above Earth, at times with weightlessness, before the reusable object landed braked by large parachutes again.
The International Aviation Federation and many other experts see 100 kilometers above Earth as a border with space, but there is no binding international regulation.
It was already Blue Origin's third manned all-excursion: At the first in July, Amazon founder Bezos himself was on board, along with his brother Mark, an 82-year-old former U.S. pilot and an 18-year-old Dutchman.
At the second in October, 90-year-old Canadian actor William Shatner, who became world famous with his role as "Captain Kirk" in "Star Trek," as well as former Nasa engineer Chris Boshuizen, entrepreneur Glen de Vries and Blue Origin's deputy boss, Audrey Powers.
Critics accuse Bezos and Blue Origin of advancing the business of space tourism largely without scientific research interests for the good of humanity and without regard to the climate.
