GDR handicraft model E-Lok E 95 03 DRG/ DR, the locomotive was in use in Silesia and central Germany, the very rare locomotive probably comes from the conversion proposal of the model railroader ME Heft 11/1968, used with game traces and missing parts see photos, function good, The series E 95 is a heavy freight train electric locomotive of the Deutsche Reichs
A locomotive is located in the DB Museum Halle (Saale) and we are operationally refurbished
In 1924, the Deutsche Reichsbahn-Gesellschaft instructed AEG to submit proposals for an electric locomotive, which on this line should be able to transport 2,200-tonne-colar trains and 530-tonne passenger trains with 45 km/h cruising speed.
65 km/h were required as the top speed.
In March 1926, the DRG thus determined the supply as 1’Co+Co1’ locomotive with AEG drive with the delivery of the mechanical part, the Siemens Schuckertwerke with the electrical part.
From December 1927 to June 1928, six locomotives were delivered, which received the operating numbers E 95 01–06.
All survived the Second World War and then found themselves on the territory of the later GDR.
In 1946 the locomotives arrived in the Soviet Union as reparations.
They returned to Germany until November 1952.
In the Reichsbahn repair plant Dessau, the reappraisability of all six locomotives was determined.
[4] When, as the central German network progressed, an increasing demand for locomotives for the heavy freight train service emerged, the three locomotives E 95 01 to E 95 03 were refurbished in 1959, whereby the remaining machines served as spare parts dispensers.
First of all, the E 04, E 44 and E 94, which are present in larger quantities, were preferably constructed.
It was only when it was foreseeable in the case of the E 94 that the number of reappraisals could not be further increased that the E 95 came into focus of reappraisal.
[4]The locomotives were initially stationed at the Leipzig-Wahren railway station.
In 1960 they came to the Halle P railway plant.
They were used in their own circulating plan mostly in front of coal trains from the Geiseltal in the direction of Halle, Leipzig and Erfurt.
With increasing age, the complicated control of the locomotives proved to be problematic.
So it often happened that Sagittarians remained glued during switching actions.
Then the Beimann, who is constantly riding along on this locomotive, had to loosen the shooter with a hammer and wooden wedge while driving.
Damage to the traction motors occurred more often and led to months-long failures on the locomotive.
[5] Since at the end of the 1960s there were enough new construction locomotives and a significant expansion of electrical operation was not planned at that time, the Reichsbahn was able to dispense with old locomotives and most splinter genres, so that the E 95 were decommissioned.
In addition, Deutsche Reichsbahn wanted to occupy electric locomotives only a few, which, due to the above-mentioned difficulties with the machines and the visibility from the driver's cab, would have revealed a problem in train operation.
[5] After mid-19969 the E 95 02 and E 95 03 were parked, they were the last to succeed the E 95 01, which occurred the service on 5 October 1970.
The intended new designation 255 001 has thus never been used.
Apart from the E 95 02, all locomotives were disassembled.
The E 95 02 has been installed as a replacement for the future museum locomotive E 77 10 as a turnout heating system on the main train station hall.
It took some time before those responsible at the Reichsbahn could be convinced that the E 95 was worth preserving as a museum locomotive.
In 1979, the initially external reappraisal of the locomotive began with the aim of being able to present it for a vehicle show in the RAW Dessau.
[5] She has since belonged to the Verkehrsmuseum Dresden as an inoperable museum locomotive.
In 1986, the locomotive was completed.
In the DB Museum Halle (Saale) the locomotive is supervised museally.
Since 1995, employees of the traditional Bw Halle P have been working on the operational reappraisal of the locomotive.
In April 2015, the locomotive was presented for the first time for an exhibition in the Museum-Bw Arnstadt with a new inner and external coating, whereby the transformers have been inspected and five driving motors are operable.
The locomotives were in use until 1945 on the Silesian mountain railway and the branching secondary routes, where they proved themselves due to their reliable propulsion and low maintenance.
In February 1945, the machines were relocated to the Central German network in Leipzig.
The main area of application was Silesia
