To invalidate fake news - Biontech-CEO releases vaccine photo
Uğur Şahin published a photo of his first vaccination on the social network "LinkedIn" in early 2021
Biontech CEO Uğur Şahin had not yet commented publicly on his vaccination status.
Now the entrepreneur posted a photo showing his corona vaccine.
The illness of a friend had moved him to do so, Şahin said.
The CEO and co-founder of the Mainz-based company Biontech, Uğur Şahin, has published a photo showing how he receives a vaccination against the coronavirus.
On social media, there has been misinformation that I'm reportedly unvaccinated.
In fact, my wife and I received 3 doses of the Biontech-Pfizer vaccine," Şahin wrote Friday in a post on the "LinkedIn" network.
"For several months now, friends have been urging me to post a photo of me vaccinating to counter these false rumours."
So far, he has rejected this, because the communication should not revolve around himself.
But a phone call last week changed his mind: "I was deeply saddened when I learned that an old school lover of mine is currently fighting for his life.
He's been battling a severe Covid-19 illness and has been attached to a ventilator for three weeks.
The school friend and his family were not vaccinated, Şahin wrote.
He didn't ask for the reasons.
"However, the most common cause appears to be anxiety, which is often stoked by misinformation and mistrust."
Anxiety is difficult to combat with facts, sometimes it is better to share personal experiences.
On the Internet, an interview circulates with Deutsche Welle in which Uğur Şahin says he is not vaccinated.
However, the video was created in December 2020, as Deutsche Welle himself made clear.
"I am currently not legally allowed to get the vaccine myself," Şahin said at the time.
There is a prioritization in vaccinating the vaccine in Germany.
Meanwhile, the prioritization has been lifted for a long time - and the Biontech boss has vaccinated three times, according to his own data.
Fire in substations: Tens of thousands of people without electricity
In several municipalities in North Rhine-Westphalia, the lights went out on Friday evening: no more electricity and in many cases probably no heating.
A fire had paralyzed a substation.
After a fire with severe damage in a substation, tens of thousands of people in the Rhein-Sieg-Kreis in North Rhine-Westphalia had to manage without electricity for around 24 hours.
As a result of the fire on Friday evening 41,000 affected residents in several municipalities, the lights went out.
Utilizer Westnetz reported that numerous emergency generators had been in operation on Saturday until the large-scale disruption could finally be completely repaired in the late evening.
Firefighters and helpers of the THW provided support in the municipalities.
Authorities offered childcare places in schools and called on neighborhood assistance.
The utilities thanked those affected for their patience and the helpers for their commitment.
We're very aware that the last 24 hours have brought significant restrictions.
We would also like to thank the affected municipalities, the district, the fire departments and the THW for the tireless efforts," Florian Witt, the head of the primary technology department, said in a statement on Saturday evening.
The repair of the damage will take a few more days, as the Westnetz installers were initially only able to temporarily put some lines back into operation, he explained.
According to the company, a technical defect led to the fire.
Electricity flows again
The fire, which erupted on Friday at around 19.15 clock, according to Westnetz, has severely damaged parts of the 10,000-volt switchgear in the Neunkirchen-Seelscheid substation.
This requires extensive cleaning and repair work.
In parallel, a connection to a 30,000-volt voltage level had been created.
With the help of this bypass, the power supply is back.
As of 6 p.m. on Saturday, over 21,000 people in Much and Ruppichteroth would have had electricity again.
Another 20,000 inhabitants of Neunkirchen-Seelscheid had been gradually switched on from 21:30.
Since 23.15 o'clock, those last affected by the failure would also have electricity.
More than 80 installers from Westnetz and partner companies were deployed.
Authorities had also called for neighborhood assistance after the construction of an emergency power supply for important buildings at noon on Saturday.
"It is now important that the citizens take care of elderly or single people in their neighborhoods and communicate with the local fire brigade equipment houses when help is needed," explained district administrator Sebastian Schuster (CDU) after a crisis meeting.
During the installation and operation of emergency generators, the focus was primarily on the residents of nursing homes.
In addition, according to the authorities, temporary childcare places have been set up in schools for those people who have not been able to care for themselves or who need electricity-dependent medical devices or are dependent on medical care.
