In order for the paint application to become even and clean, you should clean the components with a little soapy water of dust, grease and fingerprints, rinse with clear water and dry.
Before putting the brush on the model for the first time with a new color, it is advisable to practice 2 to 3 strokes.
You can do this on the inside of the components that will no longer be visible later or on a yogurt cup.
The first stroke on the component
Draw the brush evenly line beside line.
Slight overlapping of the paint layers later ensures an even surface.
Swipe back and forth quickly with the brush.
This creates unrealistic surfaces.
A model is not a garden fence.
Begin with the bright colors.
The dark ones may otherwise shine through later.
Metal tones and metallic colors come at the very end with a separate brush.
Otherwise, unsightly metal streaks can occur when they mix with normal colors.
Allow the painted parts to dry for a day before installing them.
True to the original appearance, the models reach through the decals or decals.
From sovereign signs on airplanes to ship names to ornaments and license plates for car models: The decals give the model the finishing touches.
Place the decals in the right order, as long as the components can still be easily reached.
An aircraft cockpit can be glued or more easily before it is installed in the hull.
Even more important than the right tool such as scissors, tweezers and toothpicks are time, patience and a steady hand.
Cut each decal individually and adjust it.
Decals are also called water sliding images, because you moisten their sides briefly with water, until the carrier paper gets dark through the wet.
Then you only push the picture a small piece of the paper and put the piece on the appropriate place on the model (which you have also dampened before).
As you hold the decal on the model with a tool, you pull the backing paper away on the side.
Attention: Always check whether the picture sits properly before pushing it completely off the paper, so that it does not stick, wreather or even tear on the finger.
Excess water with a household towel.
