'Half victory' against Belarus associated with high cost for the EU
According to political expert Raphael Bossong, the EU can announce a "half victory" in the conflict with the Belarusian regime.
But the costs are very high and mean massive violations of the rights of migrants and refugees to Belarus" border with Poland and Lithuania, Bossong told the Evangelical press service (epd).
The EU has not solved the confrontation, explained the researcher of the Berlin-based Stiftung Wissenschaft und Politik (SWP).
This state of limbo - Belarus doesn't admit quite small, but the EU feels as the winner - is likely to continue for a while yet.
Dealing with the people on the border is not in line with the European Convention on human rights, Bossong said.
Apparently, there is an "increasing normalization of pushbacks" by individual EU governments.
Since the EU as a whole does not resolutely oppose it, this is at the expense of fundamental rights.
At the same time, this development weakens cohesion in the Union.
"It apparently can't prevail if a member stands in the mind to do its thing," as currently Poland, which does not even allow access to the EU border protection agency Frontex.
In addition, the EU pays another price for its policy on the border with Belarus, according to Bossong: A principle agreement on asylum policy would be complicated, since the line "Border protection, border protection" had been strengthened.
Renewed attempt at border crossing
According to Poland's border guards, meanwhile migrants have again tried to reach Polish territory via Belarus.
55 migrants were arrested during the night on Sunday and returned to the border, a spokeswoman for the authority said on Sunday.
The Polish officials were pelted with stones by the migrants in the incident not far from the village of Czeremsza.
According to the information, a soldier was hit in the face and had to receive medical care by a military doctor.
In total, Border Patrol registered 52 attempted border crossings within 24 hours.
As early as Friday evening, a group of 28 migrants had overcome the border barriers.
Thousands of people from countries such as Iraq and Afghanistan had crossed the border between Belarus and the EU in recent months or tried.
The EU accuses Alexander Lukashenko, as the ruler in Minsk, of attracting them with the prospect of entering the EU.
Poland and Lithuania, according to press reports, sealed off border sections.
The EU sanctioned Belarus and threatened airlines bringing migrants and refugees into the country.
In addition, EU commissioners travelled to people’s countries of origin in order to stop departures.
The most effective element of the EU reaction is the pressure on airlines, Bossong said.
"This is where the EU has thrown its market power into balance and demonstrated its determination to operate with hard bandages and extraterritorial sanctions."
Minsk must also consider domestic political costs.
Belarusian society is probably not prepared that the security apparatus regularly smuggles thousands of people through the country.
