The break of Norwegian biathlete Ole Einar Björndalen with 94 successes is the goal of the bobsleigh pilot.
"I've factored through this, I would have to win nine and a half per season, then I could make it to Cortina 2026," said the double Olympic champion from BSC Oberbärenburg, who recorded two successes in two days at the races in Winterberg.
First on Saturday, he fended off Johannes Lochner's attack by five-hundredths, in which he drove from rank third to one.
Then he changed something about the material setting and took out a bigger lead on Sunday with 0.30 seconds left.
With his team Thorsten Margis (SV Halle), Candy Bauer (BSC Oberbärenburg) and Alexander Schüller (SV Halle), he prevailed in front of Britain's Brad Hall.
Third was the Austrian Benjamin Maier.
"We took greater risk today and were rewarded," said Friedrich, who also recorded his 22nd win in a row.
Berchtesgadener Johannes Lochner, who was driving for Stuttgart, came in fifth after finishing second earlier this time.
Christoph Hafer from BC Bad Feilnbach finished eighth after fourth place in the first race.
"If I knew what he was doing differently, I'd do it that way," Lochner said.
