What you describe is referred to as *Awareness*, i.e. sensory perceptions under general anesthesia.
These occur with a frequency of about 1 to 1000.
Most often it comes to hearing sounds/voices, less often for seeing images, and most rarely for perceiving pain.
Relevant for this topic: Caesarean sections are operations associated with an increased rate of awareness, as one avoids very deep anesthesia for the protection of the child up to the abnabling.
Preceding episodes of awareness should be mentioned during the anesthesia preliminary.
Certain anesthesia procedures, such as total intravenous anesthesia (TIVA), are associated with a slightly increased risk.
