Career in social work - as someone with a self-insecure personality disorder?
I did an apprenticeship in the field of software development and have now been trained for about a year.
Unfortunately, however, I notice more and more that this profession is not right for me, which is why I have been thinking for a long time about starting again and studying social work.
The problem: I have a self-insecure (or fearfully avoided) personality disorder (ÄVPS).
Roughly speaking, this means I don’t have self-esteem and I’m shy accordingly.
I don’t have a problem talking to people on a factual level, but it’s very difficult for me to have relaxed conversations and I’ve been told many times that I’ve seemed unapproachable (but I think I’ve gotten better).
Can I become a social worker like this?
I don’t want to work as a street worker or in an open youth club, but rather in addiction counseling or family help, for example.
But I don’t know whether the specific day-to-day work really is the way I imagine, and it’s difficult to find something on the Internet.
The ÄVPS already leads to problems in my job (for me, emotional), because it pulls me down every time when there is something to complain about about my code ("already the simplest things screwed up") and I find it difficult to ask more experienced colleagues for help ("I should really know myself how to do it by now"/"I'll keep him out of work with my stupid questions"
I’ll probably have the problems in every profession, but I wanted to mention it at this point.
