Advice and ideas on further professional life
First Merry Christmas to you!
My Christmas is unfortunately moderately good, because my dear head is crushing everything you can think about right now.
Before I get out of here .. in short: I need advice and preferably from people who somehow know my situation - so from you.
I'm doing the ref as a lateral entrant right now.
Originally I studied something different and also worked there in an area (to remain at least a little anonymous, I don't mention anything more precise here).
But the whole thing was too boring for me and I looked for alternatives that could be more fun for me.
At some point I ended up with the teaching profession and was sure it is - even if the conditions are significantly inflexible than in my previous profession, but I didn't care.
I've now arrived at the third "I'm googling other job postings."
And it's only been around for half a year.
At the paintings before, I have always decided to continue and at least end the ref, after which I can still pivot.
But right now I'm absolutely finished.
I've had so little free time the last month that I've been completely exhausted.
Now there are holidays, but there's still a lot of work waiting for me.
Before the ref I always thought I was stress-resistant, but my weekend was also my weekend and my after-house my end of work.
That's when I was vacant.
And the stress at work didn't matter in the moments.
Now even the family celebration is too much stress for me.
And then there are these rigid structures that annoy me additionally.
Basically, it's fun and viewed from the outside, the ref also runs well in terms of feedback and such.
But I'm completely exhausted and wonder how much stress my bodie endures before he shuts down completely - if he doesn't already.
I wonder if I would like to have the rest of my working life like this - especially when I read some contributions from you here - or if I shouldn't go back to the old area, where I was valued, I could take free time if I needed it, and found the work somehow boring, but was paid appropriately and worked together in a team and not as a lone fighter like that is most of the time.
But then I wonder if in a year I didn’t get back to the same place as I am now and am dissatisfied.
You think it's all kind of confusing, but you might have advice or ideas as an outsider, how I come out of this situation, which I'm completely finished and start crying at the slightest thought.
I won’t make decisions so emotionally right now, that probably doesn’t lead to anything good.
But maybe your views will help me to see at least a little light at the end of the tunnel - or to be able to enjoy Christmas, that's enough for me for now.
Thanks for reading and excuse the whiny undertone from me.
I hope my situation has become clear at least a bit.
Since its introduction in 2001, VOX's Valvetronix series has steadily evolved, which has led, among other things, to the combination of advanced modeling technology with the "Valve Reactor" circuit, which perfectly reproduces the behavior of a tubular amplifier.
With the VT20X/VT40X/VT100X Amps, we’re introducing a brand-new modeling engine that’s absolutely state-of-the-art when it comes to sound.
Thanks to VET (Virtual Element Technology) technology, which is based on the analysis of the components and amp circuits, the VTX amplifiers with an authenticity that is absolutely impressive.
Of course, these amps also contain the multi-stage Valvetronix tube preamplifier.
This is partly based on real analog circuits, because they can easily achieve the sound variations and nuances of a tube amplifier better than any simulation.
In addition to this sophisticated circuit, the VTX amplifiers offer a closed housing and a bass reflex system from which the natural resonances bubble.
VOX's "Tone Room" software allows you to even correct and bend historical facts on the virtual level until the amp and the effects sound the way you've envisioned.
The perfect combination of tube sound with modeling technology is primarily thanks to VOX Valve Reactor technology presented in 2001.
Of course, it has been refined several times since then in order to meet newly added sound requirements, which has again lead to a power boost of the VOX Valvetronix series in 2010 - with more dynamics as well as numerous and decisively improved amplifier models.
From the handy VT20+ to the VT120+ with two loudspeakers, the new VT+ Valvetronix series has also been designed in such a way that all guitarists are looking for a maximum variety of sounds here.
All guitarists dream of the pressure and the dynamics of a tube amplifier: they expect a bold sound, a nuanced reproduction of game dynamics and string stroke, as well as a prompt response.
During an in-depth analysis, the VOX engineers discovered that especially the power amplifier determines the tubular character of an amplifier and used this finding to develop the VOX Valve Reactor technology.
The Valve Reactor control of every VT+ amplifier, equipped with a 12AX7 tube, perfectly depicts the behavior, subtle nuances and sound character of a tubular amplifier.
