Volcanic eruption on La Palma: arguably longest in history
In the nearly three months since the eruption began, the lava flows on Canary Island have caused major damage.
Is there an end in sight?
With a duration of 85 days, the volcanic eruption on La Palma has been the longest in the well-known history of the Spanish Canary Island since Sunday.
Until now, the eruption of the Tehuya Volcano in 1585 with 84 days was considered to be the longest on the Atlantic island off the west coast of Africa.
There are no reliable data about previous outbursts.
The activity of the still nameless volcano in Cumbre Vieja has declined somewhat in recent days, but volcanologists continued to dare no prognosis when it could end.
Indicators indicate a slowdown.
The best news is that they're stable," island council president Mariano Hernández Zapata told state-run RTVE television.
Damages in the millions
In the nearly three months since the eruption began, the more than 1000-degree-hot lava has destroyed nearly 2900 residential homes and other buildings, RTVE reported.
Almost 1200 hectares were covered by a meter-thick lava layer.
That's equivalent to nearly 1700 football pitches.
More than 7000 inhabitants had to be evacuated, the damage is estimated at more than 900 million euros.
Off the coast, two new headlands with a size of about 50 hectares were created by colded lava.
Above all, the immensely important banana cultivation for the island, of which about half of the approximately 85,000 inhabitants lives directly or indirectly and which was in crisis even before the volcanic eruption, was badly affected.
225 hectares of banana plantations have been destroyed, further areas have been severely impainted, El País newspaper wrote.
Christmas and gifts
Christmas is just around the corner and especially the children won't be able to wait for the heavenly feast.
The many colorful gifts under the Christmas tree have something seductory combined with the spiritual atmosphere and the crackling tension.
Christmas is one of the few traditional occasions that keep our increasingly divided society together.
It is therefore all the more important to become aware of the Christmas tradition of giving, so that the gesture of tavern, which according to the French sociologist and ethnologist Marcel Mauss has been a peace-producing "societal contract of peoples of nature", escapes the rigid routine and can be filled with life again in the context of this tradition.
How Nicholas became the Christ child
Strictly speaking, it was the Protestants through whom the Christmas days were closely linked to gifting.
Previously gifts were handed to each other on St. Nicholas Day in one way or another.
The background of this still popular custom is that St. Nicholas, as Bishop of Myra, secretly threw gold into her window in one night in order to spare them another life in sin.
This unconventional way of gift-giving resulted in the custom, which is popular in Germany, to fill the boots with gifts, especially of the children.
Protestants bumped into this custom because they reject intermediaries between God and people as "holy worship."
For these reasons, they defend themselves against the institution.
Martin Luther turned St. Nicholas into the Christkind and thus founded a new line of tradition.
The focus of gifting shifted to Christmas Eve.
Since most Protestants no longer see such detailed questions so narrowly, it annoys only the fewest that the custom of St. Nicholas, similar to the custom of Martinssingen and Martin parade, is still an integral part of the pre-Christmas period.
Today you will find in boots in front of the door and under the Christmas tree from socks to coveted luxury watches everything your heart desires.
Variety of Traditions in Christian Countries
Sources prove a tradition of gifting since the 13th century on St. Nicholas Day.
With the Reformation, the day of giving shifted to Christmas Eve.
In all Christian countries, however, there is a cheerful cuddly muddle over the question of who now brings the gifts to the children.
In Germany, it depends on the respective region whether the Christkind appears on Christmas Eve or Santa Claus, who in principle is a kind of St. Nicholas, to whom over time some peculiarities have been assigned.
These include, for example, his white beard, which has little in common with the rather strict assyrian beard of the historical figure, his sleigh pulled by reindeers and the preference of the fireplace to gain access to the houses.
In the Netherlands, St. Nicholas remains the central figure on Christmas Day.
In Spain, the Holy Three Kings give the gifts, while in Italy it is the witch Befana, who is said to have shown them the way to baby Jesus.
In Scandinavia, fabulous creatures sometimes bring the gifts, while in Russia it brought Father Frost to some fame.
Here it is not reindeer, but three horses pulling his sleigh as a troika.
In the Czech Republic, the motherland of the Reformation in the course of the Hussite wars, the Christkind is responsible for handing over the gifts.
