The trade fears because of the 2G regulation for the Christmas business.
In most stores, only those who are vaccinated or recovered are allowed.
The trade thinks this is unnecessary and laments sales slumps.
He also has a possible solution ready.
From the point of view of the trade association Germany (HDE), the 2G regulations spoil the important Christmas business for the retail trade.
In the case of transactions with articles of non-daily demand, revenue slumps are to be lamented, criticized HDE CEO Stefan Genth.
According to a recent survey by the HDE among 1100 dealers, more than 70 percent of the companies looked negatively at the remaining days of the year.
Many would have already written off the Christmas business.
"What's supposed to be the positive highlight of the year is often a hole without a bottom now," Genth said.
Traders with 2G access restrictions lost almost a third of their pre-crisis sales in the past week.
It only goes well in food retailers as well as in the online sector.
The association demanded consequences from the dramatic development.
Purchasing with a mask, distance and hygiene concept is a safe matter, the chief executive said.
"2G when shopping doesn't take us any further in the fight against the pandemic," he insisted.
The current regulations deterred customers and required high personnel deployments.
The ribbon regulation must be approved nationwide, he demanded.
Thereafter, each customer would only have to be checked once for 2G (vaccinated or recovered) and would have access to all shops with the ribbon received on that day.
In addition - as in the case of public public transport in the 3G rule (vaccinated, recovered or tested) - spot checks should be sufficient.
Deutsche Euroshop, which specializes in shopping centres, also spoke of a difficult Christmas business.
The frequencies were most recently excluding 2G at a good 75 percent of the pre-corona level, the sales at about 90 percent.
These figures will now certainly fall in the middle of such an important Christmas business," CEO Wilhelm Wellner told Wirtschaftswoche.
Due to the high personnel and administrative effort, attempts are being made for the centers to find solutions and to be approved.
This includes the so-called ribbon solution, in which "visitors, for example at a central location in the center, once provide their 2G proof and thereafter - equipped with a bracelet similar to in a club hotel - can enter all shops undisturbed".
