Dampers for handball women ahead of quarter-finals against Spain
Germany's handball women sneered off the floor after humiliation against Denmark with their heads hanging and blank glances.
At the end of the World Cup maining round, the DHB selection conceded a bitter defeat at 16:32 (8:13) in front of 800 spectators in Granollers and now has to score in the quarter-finals against host Spain.
"We were far too anxious and had too much respect for the opponent," national coach Henk Groener said.
We have to perform differently against Spain.
After the first defeat in the tournament, Germany completed the main round group III with 8:2 points as second behind the Danes (10:0).
It's a pity we couldn't take the flow.
That was a bad game from everyone that we have to erase," captain Emily Bölk said.
In the fight for entry into the semi-finals against the World Cup runner-up of 2019 next Tuesday, a significant increase has to be achieved in order to keep alive the delicate dream of the first medal since 2007.
Best German thrower in the duel with the three-time Olympic champion Denmark was backroom player Alina Grijseels with six goals.
"We just weren't there - neither in attack, nor in the defense," cleared backroom player Xenia Smits, demanding: "We now have to look forward quickly".
From front to rear not at the height
In contrast than in the previous five tournament matches, the German team was not on top with the kick-off.
Misthrokes and technical errors in attack and gaps in the defense were responsible for a quick 0:4 deficit after seven minutes.
Only goalkeeper Dinah Eckerle was immediately at operating temperature and prevented worse with some parades.
The DHB team scored the first goal after 9:30 minutes when Grijseels successfully completed a quick counter-kick.
However, this was not the hoped-for initial spark.
Against the aggressive coverage of the Danes, the German women could hardly put themselves in the scene - and if they did, they mostly failed the strong Althea Reinhardt in the goal of the Scandinavians.
So the game at 3:10 after 20 minutes ran early, especially since back room player Mia Zschocke also saw red a little later because of a hard foul on her opponent.
In the quarter-finals, however, she is allowed to participate again.
Only in the final phase of the first half did the DHB selection come into the game a little better and until the break at least five goals.
The already quiet hope for a catch-up chase after the change was already gone after seven minutes at 9:19.
Again, the Groener protégés sinned in all areas and made it easy for the well-placed opponent, who is one of the medal candidates in the final round.
As a result, the German team could no longer gain an increase in performance and went off the floor as a clear loser.
