Tyre choice for road bike - really so puncture-proof?
This morning I woke up full of energy, because I wanted to train at least a little in order to prepare for my bike tour next week.
Told, done and I rode my road bike, which I bought yesterday on eBay.
The first 40km were also really nice - but then what lurked in every city center (this time: thank you Krefeld!) happened.
glass shards on the track.
Zack, loud hissing and my rear tire did not want to participate in the rest of the track anymore.
Since I had no real opportunity to get a spare coat and spare hose due to the weekday, I had to turn around again in a bad mood and take home the first track.
It wasn’t that bad in terms of mood, maybe it’s also better if I’ll practice this with the tire change again before the bike tour.
But now I'm faced with a question and I hope you can help me.
Since I have a relatively long distance over several days in front of me, I would be extremely reluctant to get this problem more often.
Now it really wasn't bad, because even the old tires from the previous owner (very nice old man, on the day I picked up the bicycle until 14 o'clock 120km!) and who had already described them as sub-optimal, because he has been riding with them for several years.
But if I think that I still have to make a tyre change when driving after 80-90kms, then I think I’m in a really bad mood :D
Now I came into conversation with an older man at the platform, who asked me wondering why I didn't have the marathon plus tyres; these are supposed to be unplatable.
Now, of course, the whole thing sounds very tempting.
But are these really so unbreakable?
I don’t want to spend 40€/tyres now, only to be disappointed.
Furthermore, I ask myself: I think I have 25x622 on the road bike at the moment.
They also (appears?) with the Marathon Plus tires, but the majority is already wider.
Is this a problem?
Or would you generally advise me on other tires on a road bike?
The Schwalbe side seems to my absolute! Laiewissen rather recommend the Durano DD?
Thank you in advance for the help and greetings!
Car huddles against tree: Driver fatally injured
The 53-year-old had bounced on the B57 between Rheinberg and Xanten with his car against a central island for an unexplained cause and then came onto the oncoming road, the police announced.
A head-on collision with the vehicle of a 27-year-old occurred there.
The 53-year-old's car was then hurled against a tree and the man trapped in the car.
The 27-year-old, as well as his 23-year-old passenger, were seriously injured in the accident and taken to surrounding hospitals.
