After the release of our new high-end wideband B 100-6 OHM, it was only a matter of time before our SOLO series would get offspring again.
SOLO 15 is the name of the new offspring and visually reminiscent of the great role models with the B 200 - 6 OHM.
However, in this construction proposal a suitably designed foot is directly included in order to lift the loudspeaker to the correct listening position.
This has no influence on the acoustics and can therefore be adapted in length according to the conditions at home.
Due to the principle, the SOLO 15 is very suitable for people in whom the maximum volume plays a rather subordinate role, but an exceptionally high level is to be reached.
A 10 cm loudspeaker is simply unable to generate such high sound pressure levels almost unfiltered.
But that doesn't mean the SOLO 15 sounds weak.
On the contrary, she performs considerably adult and extremely impressive and accurate for her size.
Tonal the SOLO 15 was matched with a slight high-frequency increase.
This is generally customary in the case of broadbands in order to optimize the rapidly increasing bundling.
Precisely aligned to the listening area, the SOLO 15 impresses with a very clear and fine sound.
Easily turned to the outside, the high tone range can be reduced and the sount pattern can be made warmer.
Thanks to the point sound source, locating the individual components of a piece of music in the optimum stereo triangle is razor-shavy and amazingly precise.
The reference-suspicious extremely low mechanical losses of the B 100 - 6 OHM ensure fine dynamics that its equivalent.
The SOLO 15 not only complements our SOLO series, but expands it in terms of dissolvability and accuracy.
As with all of our kits, it is advisable to think at the beginning about the final appearance of the loudspeaker in order to include corresponding changes during construction.
If, for example, you want to veneer the housing, the lowering of the loudspeaker has to be adjusted accordingly, since the veneer applies material again.
After all plates have been cut (e.g
With the help of a router, the loudspeaker opening in the front now follows, so that the B 100 - 6 OHM can later be inserted flush.
On the rear side, this opening should be provided with a 45° chamfer in order to make the opening surface of the loudspeaker chassis inside as large as possible.
The four screw connection points of the B 100 - 6 OHM get an interruption in the chamfer, as shown in the picture “Detail Bevel”.
Then you start with the actual construction of the box and place one of the two side walls in front of you on a flat surface.
On these, the back wall, the lid, the front and the bottom are now glued.
The three stiffeners should also be used and glued.
If required, the stiffeners themselves can vary in material thickness, provided that, for example, you want to rely on finished strips from the hardware store instead of producing them themselves from 16 mm MDF.
In the next work step, the second side is attached.
This is followed by all plates of the foot, i.e. the two sides, as well as the front.
By means of a suitable pinhole saw or a Forstner drill, the attachment of the openings of the bass reflection tube, as well as the connection clamp on the rear wall is facilitated.
Then you use a round cutter to round off all edges of the actual box.
The top side of the base plate can also be rounded.
Subsequently, the base plate is firmly glued to the foot of the housing and additionally screwed on with long wood screws.
This is followed by marking and pre-drilling (2.5 mm drillers) of the screw connection points of the loudspeaker chassis and the connection clamp.
After the housing has been cleaned of wood dust (sucking/blowing out), you start laying the cable.
This is the simplest thing possible due to the 35 mm bas reflex opening in the rear wall.
The entire and unabridged cable is pushed piece by piece through the 8 mm bore into the feet up to the terminal opening, leaving it out there about 30 cm.
In the upper part of the housing, the cable leads to the loudspeaker opening and also lets it hang out there loosely.
Subsequently, the crossover is prepared and used.
The blueprint of the crossover, as well as the corresponding connection plan, the following document can be found: Mounting instructions Weiche Solo 15.
If the crossover is installed and the connection terminal is screwed together, the sealing of the 8 mm cable opening in the interior of the box follows, which is not yet completely closed.
Due to the 35 mm bASSREFLEX opening and with a little skill, you can drip hot glue to the opening and thus close.
Through the opening of the B 100 - 6 OHM and with a small lamp you can check the seal accordingly.
Now follows the dampening according to the description.
Then the B 100-6 OHM is connected and screwed tight with the corresponding screws.
The polarity plays an important role here, so that the positive line (marked cable) has to be soldered at the positive pole of the loudspeaker (larger contact).
Finally, the bass reflex tube is shortened and plugged in to the specified length of 28 mm (total length of the tube).
For damping, a mat of the damping material is cut into two parts and loosely distributed in the housing.
The opening around the bass reflection channel should be kept free.
